E.append(topic("topic-neural-development-malformations", "Neural development and malformations", "development",
 "The nervous system forms from the neural plate by neurulation, segmentation of the neural tube into the primary and secondary brain vesicles, proliferation of neurons in the ventricular zone, radial migration to build the cortex, and the late events of synapse formation and myelination that continue into adult life. Each step has a characteristic failure: a defect of closure gives spina bifida or anencephaly, a defect of prosencephalic cleavage gives holoprosencephaly, a defect of proliferation gives microcephaly, a defect of migration gives lissencephaly, heterotopia and polymicrogyria, and a defect of the posterior fossa gives Chiari and Dandy-Walker malformations. Knowing the timetable turns a malformation into a date of injury.",
 [("Neurulation and the neural tube", "The neural plate folds during the third and fourth weeks and closes first in the cervical region, then zips toward the anterior neuropore (closing around day 25) and the posterior neuropore (day 27). Failure at the anterior end produces anencephaly and encephalocele; failure at the posterior end produces the spectrum of spina bifida from occulta to myelomeningocele, in which the open cord is exposed on the back and almost always accompanied by a Chiari II malformation and hydrocephalus. Folate before conception reduces the risk, and maternal serum alpha-fetoprotein and second-trimester ultrasound detect the open defects. The neural crest, which separates from the closing tube, gives rise to the dorsal root and autonomic ganglia, Schwann cells, the adrenal medulla and the meninges of the cord, so crest disorders combine peripheral nerve, pigment and craniofacial findings."),
  ("Vesicles, flexures and the ventricular system", "The rostral tube expands into the prosencephalon, mesencephalon and rhombencephalon, then into the telencephalon and diencephalon, the midbrain, and the metencephalon (pons and cerebellum) and myelencephalon (medulla); the lumen of the tube becomes the ventricles and the aqueduct, and the flexures bend the axis so that the cerebral hemispheres come to lie above the brainstem. Failure of the prosencephalon to divide into two hemispheres is holoprosencephaly, ranging from a single ventricle with cyclopia to a mild form with only a missing septum pellucidum and midline facial change, associated with sonic hedgehog pathway defects and trisomy 13. Aqueductal stenosis, from malformation or infection, is the commonest cause of congenital hydrocephalus."),
  ("Proliferation and migration of cortical neurons", "Neurons are born in the ventricular and subventricular zones between the seventh and sixteenth weeks and migrate along radial glia to the cortical plate, the later-born neurons passing the earlier ones to form the six layers inside-out; interneurons arrive tangentially from the ganglionic eminences. Reduced proliferation yields microcephaly (genetic, or from congenital infection, alcohol or radiation); arrested migration yields lissencephaly with a thick, smooth four-layered cortex (LIS1, DCX), band and periventricular nodular heterotopia in which neurons stop in the white matter or beside the ventricle (FLNA), and abnormal late organization yields polymicrogyria and focal cortical dysplasia, the commonest structural causes of childhood epilepsy. The timing of the insult, not its nature, decides the pattern."),
  ("Posterior fossa, spinal cord and myelination", "The cerebellum forms from the rhombic lips of the metencephalon and the vermis fuses in the midline late in the first trimester; failure gives the Dandy-Walker malformation with a cystic fourth ventricle and vermian hypoplasia, and a small posterior fossa crowds the tonsils downward as the Chiari malformations. The spinal cord ends at the level of the third lumbar vertebra at birth and rises to L1-L2 by adulthood because the vertebral column grows faster; failure of the filum to lengthen tethers the cord and produces progressive leg and bladder symptoms in childhood. Myelination proceeds from the brainstem outward and from sensory to motor systems, finishing in the frontal association areas in the third decade, which is why an infant's Babinski sign is normal and why white-matter diseases of childhood are staged by the myelination that should have occurred.")],
 ["Neural tube closure fails in the fourth week: anencephaly at the front, spina bifida at the back, both reduced by preconception folate", "The pattern of a malformation dates the insult: proliferation (microcephaly) before migration (lissencephaly, heterotopia) before organization (polymicrogyria)", "Holoprosencephaly is a failure of midline cleavage and is often paired with midline facial defects", "Chiari II accompanies myelomeningocele; Chiari I is an adult finding with a small posterior fossa", "Tethered cord presents with growth: leg weakness, foot deformity and bladder symptoms appear as the child lengthens"],
 ["A newborn with an open back has a brain problem too: hydrocephalus and Chiari II are part of the package", "Migration disorders are epilepsy disorders; look for them on the MRI of every child with refractory seizures", "The cord ends at L1-L2 in the adult and L3 in the newborn: choose the puncture level accordingly"],
 [R("sp-neural-tube-development-and-stages"), R("sp-malformations"), R("sp-neural-tube")], meshIds=["ventricle-lateral-l", "ventricle-lateral-r", "ventricle-fourth", "cerebellar-vermis-vi", "brainstem"],
 imaging=("Fetal and neonatal ultrasound and MRI stage the malformation; in children and adults T1 and T2 volumetric MRI show gyral pattern, cortical thickness, gray matter in the wrong place (heterotopia) and posterior fossa anatomy.",
  [pathol("Lissencephaly", "MRI", "Smooth cortex with few shallow sulci, thick cortical band and a figure-of-eight hemispheric shape; band heterotopia shows a second layer of gray matter beneath a thin cortex.", sequence="T1"),
   pathol("Periventricular nodular heterotopia", "MRI", "Nodules isointense to gray matter lining the lateral ventricles, often bilateral, in a patient with seizures beginning in adolescence.", sequence="T1"),
   pathol("Chiari II with myelomeningocele", "MRI", "Small posterior fossa with the vermis and medulla displaced below the foramen magnum, tectal beaking, hydrocephalus and a lumbosacral spinal defect.", sequence="T2"),
   pathol("Dandy-Walker malformation", "MRI", "Cystic dilatation of the fourth ventricle with hypoplasia and upward rotation of the vermis and an enlarged posterior fossa.", sequence="T2")]),
 structures=["ventricle-lateral", "ventricle-fourth", "cerebellar-vermis", "conus-medullaris", "corpus-callosum"], syndromes=["syn-chiari-tonsillar-ectopia", "syn-hydrocephalus"], topics=["topic-pediatric-neurological-syndromes", "topic-csf-physiology-blood-brain-barrier"],
 pitfalls=["Gray matter in the white matter on MRI is heterotopia, not tumor; it follows gray matter signal on every sequence and does not enhance"], tags=["development", "malformation", "neurulation"]))

E.append(topic("topic-csf-physiology-blood-brain-barrier", "CSF physiology and the blood–brain barrier", "physiology",
 "Cerebrospinal fluid is secreted by the choroid plexus of the ventricles at about half a milliliter a minute, flows through the ventricular system into the subarachnoid space by the foramina of the fourth ventricle, and is absorbed into the venous sinuses through the arachnoid granulations and along the cranial and spinal nerve sheaths; the whole volume of about 150 ml turns over three or four times a day. The blood–brain barrier of tight-junctioned capillary endothelium and the blood–CSF barrier of the choroid epithelium keep the extracellular fluid of the brain constant and exclude most drugs and proteins, and both are breached by inflammation, tumor and infarction. These systems explain hydrocephalus, intracranial hypertension, the composition of a normal and an abnormal spinal fluid, and why contrast enhancement means disease.",
 [("Production, circulation and absorption", "The choroid plexus in the lateral, third and fourth ventricles secretes CSF actively, driven by carbonic anhydrase and the sodium pump of the choroid epithelium (the target of acetazolamide), at a rate that is largely independent of pressure; the fluid passes through the interventricular foramina, the third ventricle, the aqueduct and the fourth ventricle, and leaves through the median foramen of Magendie and the lateral foramina of Luschka into the cisterna magna and the basal cisterns. It flows over the convexity to the arachnoid granulations, where a pressure gradient of a few centimeters of water moves it into the superior sagittal sinus, and along the olfactory and spinal nerve roots to lymphatics; a glymphatic route along perivascular spaces clears interstitial solutes during sleep. Absorption rises with pressure, so a fixed increase in production produces only a modest rise in pressure; obstruction of flow or absorption produces hydrocephalus."),
  ("Hydrocephalus and intracranial pressure", "Obstruction within the ventricles or at the fourth ventricular outlets (tumor, aqueductal stenosis, hemorrhage, Chiari) produces non-communicating hydrocephalus with enlargement of the ventricles above the block; impaired absorption at the granulations after subarachnoid hemorrhage, meningitis or venous sinus thrombosis produces communicating hydrocephalus in which all ventricles dilate. Normal-pressure hydrocephalus of the elderly combines ventriculomegaly with a triad of gait apraxia, incontinence and dementia and improves with shunting when a large-volume tap improves the gait. Intracranial pressure is normally below 15 mmHg; because the skull is closed, the Monro-Kellie principle means that any added volume of blood, CSF, edema or mass must displace another, and when compensation fails pressure rises steeply and herniation follows. Idiopathic intracranial hypertension raises CSF pressure without a mass or ventricular enlargement and threatens vision through papilledema."),
  ("Composition and lumbar puncture", "Normal CSF is clear, with fewer than five lymphocytes per microliter, protein below 0.45 g/L, glucose about two-thirds of the plasma value and an opening pressure of 10 to 20 cm of water in the lateral position. Neutrophils, low glucose and high protein mean bacterial meningitis; lymphocytes with normal glucose mean viral meningitis or encephalitis; lymphocytes with low glucose mean tuberculosis, fungi or carcinomatosis; red cells that do not clear between the first and last tube, with xanthochromia after twelve hours, mean subarachnoid hemorrhage; and oligoclonal bands present in CSF but not serum mean intrathecal immunoglobulin synthesis, the laboratory signature of multiple sclerosis. The puncture is made below the conus, at L3-L4 or L4-L5, and is dangerous when a mass or obstructive hydrocephalus creates a pressure gradient across the tentorium or the foramen magnum."),
  ("The barriers and their failure", "Brain capillaries have continuous tight junctions, few vesicles and a sheath of astrocyte end-feet and pericytes, so lipid-soluble molecules, gases and substances with transporters (glucose, amino acids, levodopa) enter while proteins, most antibiotics, dopamine and many drugs do not; the choroid plexus epithelium forms the blood–CSF barrier with the same selectivity. The circumventricular organs (area postrema, median eminence, organum vasculosum, subfornical organ, pineal and posterior pituitary) have fenestrated capillaries and sense or secrete into the blood. The barrier opens in inflammation, tumor, infarction and hypertensive crisis, which is why gadolinium and iodinated contrast enhance those lesions, why vasogenic edema spreads through the white matter around them, why antibiotics reach the CSF only when the meninges are inflamed and why osmotic agents such as mannitol shrink the normal brain more than the injured part. Immature barriers in the neonate let bilirubin reach the basal ganglia in kernicterus.")],
 ["CSF is made at 0.5 ml per minute and turned over three to four times a day; absorption, not production, sets the pressure", "Non-communicating hydrocephalus blocks the ventricles; communicating hydrocephalus blocks the granulations; both enlarge the temporal horns first", "Contrast enhancement means the blood–brain barrier is open: tumor, inflammation, infection or subacute infarction", "Neutrophils and low glucose in CSF mean bacterial meningitis; lymphocytes with low glucose mean tuberculosis, fungus or cancer", "The Monro-Kellie doctrine makes every added milliliter in the skull someone else's problem"],
 ["Measure the opening pressure at every lumbar puncture; a normal CSF with high pressure is idiopathic intracranial hypertension or venous thrombosis", "Never puncture below an obstruction you have not imaged", "Acetazolamide works on the choroid plexus, which is why it treats intracranial hypertension"],
 [R("sp-cerebrospinal-fluid"), R("sp-blood-brain-barrier-2"), R("sp-blood-brain-barrier")], meshIds=["ventricle-lateral-l", "ventricle-lateral-r", "ventricle-third", "ventricle-fourth"],
 imaging=("Ventricular size relative to the sulci and the temporal horns distinguishes hydrocephalus from atrophy; CSF flow studies show aqueductal flow; contrast enhancement maps barrier breakdown.",
  [pathol("Obstructive hydrocephalus", "MRI", "Dilated lateral and third ventricles with a normal fourth ventricle in aqueductal stenosis; periventricular FLAIR halo of transependymal CSF in acute cases.", sequence="T2"),
   pathol("Normal-pressure hydrocephalus", "MRI", "Ventriculomegaly out of proportion to sulcal atrophy with tight high-convexity sulci and an enlarged Sylvian fissure (DESH pattern).", sequence="T1"),
   pathol("Idiopathic intracranial hypertension", "MRV", "Empty sella, flattened posterior globes, distended optic nerve sheaths and transverse sinus stenosis on MRV, with normal ventricles.", sequence="n/a"),
   pathol("Barrier breakdown with vasogenic edema", "MRI", "Ring or solid enhancement after gadolinium with finger-like FLAIR edema spreading through the white matter around a tumor or abscess.", sequence="T1+Gd")]),
 structures=["choroid-plexus", "arachnoid-granulations", "ventricle-lateral", "cerebral-aqueduct", "area-postrema", "subarachnoid-space-cisterns"], syndromes=["syn-hydrocephalus", "syn-normal-pressure-hydrocephalus", "syn-idiopathic-intracranial-hypertension", "syn-subarachnoid-hemorrhage"], topics=["topic-neuroimaging-basics", "topic-cns-infections-localization"],
 pitfalls=["Ventricles enlarge with atrophy too; hydrocephalus needs the temporal horns rounded and the sulci tight, not just big ventricles"], tags=["CSF", "blood-brain barrier", "hydrocephalus"]))

E.append(topic("topic-neurotransmitter-systems", "Neurotransmitter systems", "neurochemistry",
 "The brain uses a small number of transmitters in two organizations: the point-to-point synapses of glutamate (excitatory) and GABA and glycine (inhibitory) that carry the signals of every pathway in this atlas, and the diffuse modulatory systems of dopamine, noradrenaline, serotonin, acetylcholine and histamine that arise from small brainstem and basal forebrain nuclei and set the state of the whole brain. Peptides, purines and endocannabinoids modulate both. Clinical neurology is largely the pharmacology of these systems: parkinsonism is dopamine loss, myasthenia is a cholinergic synapse under attack, epilepsy is an excess of glutamate over GABA, anticholinergic delirium and serotonin syndrome are drug effects on the modulatory nuclei, and antidepressants, antipsychotics, anesthetics and antiepileptics are all transmitter drugs.",
 [("Fast transmission: glutamate, GABA and glycine", "Glutamate is the transmitter of the corticospinal tract, the thalamocortical and corticocortical projections, the sensory afferents and the cerebellar mossy and climbing fibers; it acts through AMPA receptors for fast depolarization and NMDA receptors that require depolarization and glutamate together and let calcium in, the coincidence detector of synaptic plasticity and the mediator of excitotoxic death in stroke, status epilepticus and hypoglycemia. GABA is released by cortical interneurons, striatal and pallidal projection neurons, Purkinje cells and the reticular thalamic nucleus, opening chloride channels (GABA-A, the site of benzodiazepines, barbiturates and most anesthetics) or potassium channels (GABA-B, the site of baclofen). Glycine is the inhibitory transmitter of the spinal cord and brainstem; its blockade by strychnine or tetanus toxin and its receptor mutations in hyperekplexia produce rigidity and exaggerated startle. Seizures are a failure of the balance between these systems."),
  ("Dopamine and noradrenaline", "Dopamine neurons of the substantia nigra pars compacta project to the striatum (nigrostriatal), and those of the ventral tegmental area to the accumbens, amygdala and prefrontal cortex (mesolimbic and mesocortical); the tuberoinfundibular neurons of the hypothalamus inhibit prolactin. Loss of the nigrostriatal system produces parkinsonism, excess mesolimbic activity produces psychosis and addiction, and dopamine antagonists produce parkinsonism, dystonia, akathisia, tardive dyskinesia and hyperprolactinemia, while agonists produce hallucinations and impulse control disorders. Noradrenaline arises almost entirely from the locus coeruleus and drives arousal, attention, the stress response and the descending inhibition of pain; its loss contributes to the sleep and autonomic symptoms of Parkinson disease, and drugs that raise it (stimulants, some antidepressants) improve attention and raise blood pressure. Peripheral noradrenaline is the sympathetic transmitter, and dopamine-beta-hydroxylase deficiency abolishes it."),
  ("Serotonin, acetylcholine and histamine", "Serotonin from the raphe nuclei modulates mood, anxiety, appetite, sleep, pain and the trigeminovascular system, which is why triptans and reuptake inhibitors act where they do and why combining serotonergic drugs produces the clonus, hyperthermia and agitation of serotonin syndrome. Acetylcholine is the transmitter of motor neurons at the neuromuscular junction (nicotinic receptors, the target of myasthenia antibodies, curare and botulinum toxin), of all preganglionic and parasympathetic postganglionic autonomic neurons (nicotinic and muscarinic), of the basal forebrain projection to the cortex and hippocampus that sustains attention and memory (lost in Alzheimer and Lewy body disease), and of the pedunculopontine and striatal interneuron systems; anticholinergic drugs cause delirium, and cholinesterase inhibitors treat both myasthenia and dementia. Histamine from the tuberomammillary nucleus promotes wakefulness, the reason sedating antihistamines sedate."),
  ("Transmitter disorders at the bedside", "A transmitter system can be recognized by its clinical signature: excess dopamine gives psychosis and chorea, deficiency gives parkinsonism; excess cholinergic activity gives salivation, lacrimation, urination, diarrhea, bronchospasm and fasciculations (organophosphates), deficiency gives dry mouth, dilated pupils, urinary retention and delirium; excess serotonin gives clonus and hyperthermia; loss of GABA gives seizures and the withdrawal syndrome of alcohol and benzodiazepines; and NMDA receptor antibodies give psychosis, seizures, dyskinesia and autonomic instability in anti-NMDA receptor encephalitis. Neuromuscular junction disorders divide by the synapse: presynaptic (Lambert-Eaton, botulism) versus postsynaptic (myasthenia gravis). Drug histories should be read as a list of transmitter manipulations.")],
 ["Glutamate and GABA carry the signal; the monoamines and acetylcholine set the state", "Parkinsonism is dopamine loss, psychosis is dopamine excess, and the same drugs move patients between the two", "Anticholinergic delirium, serotonin syndrome and neuroleptic malignant syndrome are transmitter emergencies that mimic infection", "The neuromuscular junction is the most accessible synapse: presynaptic calcium channels (Lambert-Eaton), vesicle release (botulism), postsynaptic receptors (myasthenia)", "NMDA receptors couple depolarization to calcium entry: plasticity when brief, cell death when prolonged"],
 ["Read every drug list as a transmitter list before blaming the brain", "Cholinesterase inhibitors help attention more than memory because the nucleus basalis dies first", "A patient on two serotonergic drugs with clonus and fever has serotonin syndrome, not sepsis, until proved otherwise"],
 [R("sp-neurotransmitters")], meshIds=["substantia-nigra-pars-compacta-l", "substantia-nigra-pars-compacta-r", "ventral-tegmental-area-l", "ventral-tegmental-area-r", "raphe-dorsal", "nucleus-basalis-meynert-l", "nucleus-basalis-meynert-r"],
 imaging=("Transmitter systems are imaged functionally: dopamine transporter SPECT for the nigrostriatal pathway, neuromelanin MRI for the nigra and locus coeruleus, PET ligands for cholinergic and serotonergic terminals.",
  [pathol("Nigrostriatal dopamine loss", "SPECT", "Reduced dopamine transporter uptake in the putamen, asymmetric and posterior-predominant, in Parkinson disease and Lewy body dementia; normal in essential tremor and drug-induced parkinsonism.", sequence="n/a"),
   pathol("Locus coeruleus and nigra degeneration", "MRI", "Loss of neuromelanin signal in the locus coeruleus and substantia nigra pars compacta on neuromelanin-sensitive T1 images.", sequence="T1"),
   pathol("Anti-NMDA receptor encephalitis", "MRI", "Often normal or with subtle medial temporal FLAIR change despite severe psychiatric, seizure and autonomic disturbance; CSF antibodies make the diagnosis.", sequence="FLAIR")]),
 structures=["substantia-nigra-pars-compacta", "ventral-tegmental-area", "raphe-dorsal", "locus-coeruleus", "nucleus-basalis-meynert", "hypothalamus-tuberomammillary", "neuromuscular-junction"], syndromes=["syn-parkinsonism", "syn-myasthenia-gravis", "syn-lambert-eaton", "syn-huntington-chorea"], pathways=["pathway-basal-ganglia-loops"], topics=["topic-movement-disorder-circuits", "topic-sleep-eeg-basics", "topic-neuromuscular-patterns"],
 pitfalls=["A normal dopamine transporter scan in a parkinsonian patient means the problem is downstream (drug-induced, vascular or functional), not in the nigra"], tags=["neurotransmitters", "pharmacology", "synapse"]))

E.append(topic("topic-sleep-eeg-basics", "Sleep, arousal and EEG basics", "electrophysiology",
 "Wakefulness is maintained by the ascending arousal system of the brainstem and hypothalamus, cholinergic, monoaminergic and orexinergic, which drives the thalamus and cortex, and sleep is imposed by the preoptic hypothalamus, which switches those systems off, with the REM state generated in the pons. The electroencephalogram records the summed synaptic currents of cortical pyramidal cells, paced by thalamocortical loops, and its rhythms map these states: alpha in relaxed wakefulness, theta and spindles in light sleep, delta in deep sleep, low-voltage fast activity with eye movements and atonia in REM. Disorders of this machinery, narcolepsy, REM behavior disorder, insomnia after hypothalamic lesions, coma from brainstem damage and the encephalopathies that slow the EEG, are read directly from the physiology.",
 [("The ascending arousal system", "Cholinergic neurons of the pedunculopontine and laterodorsal tegmental nuclei activate the thalamus, and monoaminergic neurons of the locus coeruleus (noradrenaline), the raphe (serotonin), the tuberomammillary nucleus (histamine) and the ventral tegmental area (dopamine) together with the basal forebrain cholinergic neurons activate the cortex directly; the orexin neurons of the lateral hypothalamus stabilize the whole system in the waking state. Damage to the paramedian upper pons and midbrain, where these pathways converge, produces coma, and bilateral paramedian thalamic infarction produces hypersomnolence, whereas hemispheric lesions cause coma only when they compress the brainstem. Loss of the orexin neurons produces narcolepsy, in which the wake state collapses into sleep and REM intrudes into waking as cataplexy, sleep paralysis and hypnagogic hallucinations."),
  ("The sleep switch and the sleep cycle", "The ventrolateral and median preoptic nuclei inhibit every arousal nucleus with GABA and galanin at sleep onset and are inhibited by them during waking, a mutually inhibitory flip-flop that gives sharp transitions and is biased by the homeostatic pressure of adenosine (blocked by caffeine) and the circadian signal from the suprachiasmatic nucleus through the dorsomedial hypothalamus. Non-REM sleep deepens through stages N1 to N3 as thalamic neurons hyperpolarize and generate spindles and then delta waves; REM sleep is generated by the sublaterodorsal nucleus of the pons, which produces cortical activation, rapid eye movements and, through the medullary reticular formation, the atonia of skeletal muscle. Cycles of 90 minutes repeat four to six times, with REM lengthening toward morning. Loss of REM atonia produces dream enactment, the REM sleep behavior disorder that precedes synucleinopathies by years."),
  ("Reading the EEG", "Scalp electrodes record 10 to 100 microvolt fluctuations produced by synchronous postsynaptic potentials in the apical dendrites of cortical pyramidal cells; the rhythms depend on thalamocortical pacing and cortical health. Normal waking shows a posterior alpha rhythm of 8 to 13 Hz that attenuates with eye opening, drowsiness shows theta, N2 sleep shows sleep spindles and K-complexes, and N3 shows high-amplitude delta; REM shows low-voltage fast activity with sawtooth waves. Diffuse slowing indicates encephalopathy of any cause, triphasic waves suggest metabolic or hepatic encephalopathy, focal slowing indicates a structural lesion, periodic lateralized discharges indicate an acute cortical injury such as herpes encephalitis or stroke, generalized periodic complexes in a demented patient suggest Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease, and burst suppression or electrocerebral silence indicate deep anesthesia or catastrophic injury. Epileptiform spikes and sharp waves are the subject of the epilepsy entry."),
  ("Clinical disorders of sleep and arousal", "Narcolepsy type 1 is orexin deficiency with cataplexy and low CSF hypocretin; idiopathic hypersomnia and sleep apnea are the other causes of daytime sleepiness, and the multiple sleep latency test with a preceding polysomnogram separates them. REM sleep behavior disorder is a pontine sign and predicts Parkinson disease, Lewy body dementia or multiple system atrophy. Insomnia follows preoptic and anterior hypothalamic lesions; circadian disorders follow suprachiasmatic damage and blindness. Coma reflects damage to the arousal system or bilateral hemispheric dysfunction, and its grading, the brainstem reflexes and the EEG pattern determine prognosis; the persistent vegetative state retains sleep-wake cycles without awareness, and the locked-in state retains both. Sedative and antihistamine drugs act on these same nuclei, which is why they cause both sleep and delirium.")],
 ["Coma needs a lesion of the paramedian upper brainstem or of both hemispheres; a single hemisphere cannot produce it without herniation", "Narcolepsy is orexin loss: sleep attacks, cataplexy and REM intrusions", "REM sleep behavior disorder is a pontine sign that predicts synuclein disease years before parkinsonism", "The posterior alpha rhythm that blocks with eye opening is the first thing to find on a normal EEG", "Triphasic waves, periodic lateralized discharges and burst suppression each tell a different story: metabolic, focal cortical injury, catastrophic"],
 ["Ask about dream enactment in every patient with parkinsonism or dementia: the answer dates the disease", "A slow EEG says encephalopathy, not which one; the history and the metabolic screen say which", "Sedatives and antihistamines act on the same nuclei that produce delirium in the elderly"],
 [R("sp-eeg-normal-sleep"), R("sp-sleep-stages"), R("sp-reticular-activating-system")], meshIds=["raphe-dorsal", "pedunculopontine-nucleus-l", "pedunculopontine-nucleus-r", "hypothalamus-lateral-l", "hypothalamus-lateral-r", "hypothalamus-suprachiasmatic-l", "hypothalamus-suprachiasmatic-r", "thalamus-pulvinar-medial-centrolateral-l", "thalamus-pulvinar-medial-centrolateral-r"],
 imaging=("Sleep disorders are diagnosed by polysomnography and the multiple sleep latency test; MRI is reserved for secondary causes (hypothalamic, pontine and thalamic lesions) and coma.",
  [pathol("Paramedian thalamic and midbrain infarct with hypersomnolence", "MRI", "Bilateral paramedian thalamic restricted diffusion with or without a midbrain lesion (artery of Percheron).", sequence="DWI"),
   pathol("Pontine tegmental lesion with REM disturbance", "MRI", "Lesion of the dorsolateral pontine tegmentum (infarct, demyelination, tumor) in a patient with new dream enactment or cataplexy.", sequence="FLAIR"),
   pathol("Hypothalamic lesion with narcolepsy or insomnia", "MRI", "Mass, inflammation or infarct of the lateral or anterior hypothalamus with secondary narcolepsy or loss of sleep.", sequence="T1+Gd")]),
 structures=["reticular-formation", "locus-coeruleus", "raphe-nuclei", "hypothalamus-lateral", "hypothalamus-medial-preoptic", "hypothalamus-suprachiasmatic", "hypothalamus-tuberomammillary", "thalamus"], syndromes=["syn-top-of-basilar", "syn-locked-in", "syn-central-transtentorial-herniation"], topics=["topic-epilepsy-localization", "topic-neurotransmitter-systems", "topic-consciousness-coma-brain-death"],
 pitfalls=["Daytime sleepiness in a snorer is sleep apnea until a sleep study says otherwise; do not label it narcolepsy"], tags=["sleep", "EEG", "arousal"]))

E.append(topic("topic-epilepsy-localization", "Epilepsy: seizure semiology and localization", "disease-pattern",
 "A seizure is a transient burst of abnormally synchronized cortical activity, and its clinical expression is a map of the cortex it passes through: the first symptom (the aura) names the site of onset, the sequence of later symptoms traces the spread, and the postictal deficits mark the cortex that was exhausted. Focal seizures arise in one region and may spread to both hemispheres; generalized seizures begin in bilateral thalamocortical networks. The atlas structures involved, the medial temporal lobe, the frontal motor and supplementary motor areas, the insula, the occipital lobe and the thalamus, each produce a recognizable semiology, and the interictal and ictal EEG, the MRI and the neuropsychology converge on the epileptogenic zone that surgery can remove.",
 [("Temporal lobe seizures", "Medial temporal seizures, the commonest focal epilepsy of adults, begin with a rising epigastric sensation, fear, deja vu or an olfactory or gustatory hallucination from the amygdala, hippocampus and uncus, then impair awareness with a motionless stare, oral automatisms (lip smacking, chewing) and hand automatisms ipsilateral to the focus with dystonic posturing of the contralateral hand; they last one to three minutes and are followed by confusion and, with dominant-side onset, postictal aphasia. Lateral temporal seizures begin with auditory hallucinations, vertigo or complex visual scenes. The substrate is usually hippocampal sclerosis, a dysembryoplastic tumor, a cavernoma or a dysplasia, and anterior temporal lobectomy renders most patients seizure-free when MRI, EEG and neuropsychology agree."),
  ("Frontal lobe seizures", "Frontal seizures are brief, frequent, nocturnal and often bizarre: supplementary motor area seizures produce sudden asymmetric tonic posturing with the contralateral arm abducted and the head turned toward it (the fencing posture) and vocalization with preserved awareness; primary motor cortex seizures produce clonic jerking that marches along the homunculus (the Jacksonian march) and leave a Todd paresis; frontal eye field seizures turn the head and eyes away from the focus; prefrontal and cingulate seizures produce hypermotor thrashing, pedaling, screaming and fear; opercular seizures produce salivation, throat sensations and speech arrest. Scalp EEG is often normal or obscured by movement, and these seizures are frequently misdiagnosed as parasomnias or psychogenic attacks until video-EEG shows their stereotypy."),
  ("Parietal, occipital and insular seizures, and generalized epilepsies", "Parietal seizures begin with contralateral tingling, pain, a sense of a missing or moving limb or vertigo; occipital seizures begin with elementary colored visual hallucinations in the contralateral field, ictal blindness or eye deviation, and spread forward to temporal or parietal semiology; insular seizures produce laryngeal constriction, paresthesias and autonomic changes and mimic temporal seizures. Generalized epilepsies (absence, myoclonic, generalized tonic-clonic) begin in thalamocortical circuits with generalized spike-wave discharges: absence seizures are brief stares with 3 Hz spike-wave, juvenile myoclonic epilepsy adds morning jerks and photosensitivity, and the choice of drug differs from focal epilepsy, some sodium channel blockers worsening absence and myoclonus. Status epilepticus, a seizure over five minutes or repeated without recovery, is a medical emergency treated with benzodiazepines then a loading antiepileptic."),
  ("Localizing the focus for treatment", "The history establishes the aura and the spread; interictal EEG shows spikes over the focus (anterior temporal spikes in medial temporal epilepsy, obscured or midline spikes in frontal epilepsy); MRI with thin coronal FLAIR and T1 through the hippocampi finds sclerosis, dysplasia and tumors; FDG-PET shows interictal hypometabolism of the focus, and ictal SPECT shows hyperperfusion; neuropsychology lateralizes memory and language; and when these disagree, intracranial recording with depth electrodes maps the onset. Lateralizing signs during the seizure include ipsilateral automatisms with contralateral dystonia, head version away from the focus at the start of a bilateral tonic-clonic seizure, postictal aphasia (dominant) and postictal nose wiping with the ipsilateral hand. The treatment ladder runs from drugs to resective surgery, laser ablation, vagus nerve or responsive stimulation and thalamic stimulation.")],
 ["The aura is the address: epigastric and fear for the medial temporal lobe, colored shapes for the occipital lobe, a tonic fencing posture for the supplementary motor area", "Dystonic posturing of a hand during a seizure points to the opposite hemisphere; automatisms with it point to the same side", "Frontal seizures are short, nocturnal and strange, and the EEG is often normal", "Hippocampal sclerosis is the commonest surgically curable epilepsy; look at the hippocampi on coronal FLAIR", "Absence and myoclonic epilepsies are generalized and are worsened by some drugs that help focal seizures"],
 ["A first seizure after 25 is focal until proved otherwise: image the brain", "Ask the witness what happened first, not what happened most", "Todd paresis after a seizure localizes the motor cortex and can mimic a stroke for hours"],
 [R("sp-localization-related-epilepsies-on-eeg"), R("sp-partial-epilepsy"), R("sp-seizure")], meshIds=["hippocampus-l", "hippocampus-r", "amygdala-l", "amygdala-r", "cortex-supplementary-motor-l", "cortex-supplementary-motor-r", "gyrus-precentral-l", "gyrus-precentral-r", "insula-l", "insula-r", "cortex-intracalcarine-l", "cortex-intracalcarine-r"],
 imaging=("An epilepsy MRI protocol includes thin coronal T2/FLAIR and volumetric T1 perpendicular to the hippocampal axis, T2* or SWI for cavernomas and hemosiderin, and post-contrast images when a tumor is possible; FDG-PET and ictal SPECT add localizing evidence.",
  [pathol("Mesial temporal sclerosis", "MRI", "Small hippocampus with loss of internal architecture and increased FLAIR signal, often with ipsilateral fornix and mammillary body atrophy and a dilated temporal horn.", sequence="FLAIR"),
   pathol("Focal cortical dysplasia", "MRI", "Cortical thickening, blurred gray-white junction and a transmantle band of FLAIR signal tapering toward the ventricle; often subtle and missed without thin slices.", sequence="FLAIR"),
   pathol("Dysembryoplastic neuroepithelial tumor or ganglioglioma", "MRI", "Cortically based multicystic or bubbly T2-bright lesion of the temporal lobe with little edema and little or no enhancement.", sequence="T2"),
   pathol("Cavernoma", "MRI", "Popcorn-like lesion with a hemosiderin rim on T2 and blooming on SWI, without edema unless recently bled.", sequence="SWI")]),
 structures=["hippocampus", "amygdala", "uncus", "cortex-supplementary-motor", "gyrus-precentral", "frontal-eye-field", "insula", "cortex-intracalcarine", "thalamus"], syndromes=["syn-uncal-herniation"], topics=["topic-sleep-eeg-basics", "topic-neural-development-malformations", "topic-brain-tumor-localization"],
 pitfalls=["Convulsive syncope with a few jerks is not epilepsy; the setting, the prodrome and the rapid recovery separate them", "Psychogenic attacks coexist with epilepsy in a fifth of patients; video-EEG, not the story, decides"], tags=["epilepsy", "seizure", "semiology"]))

E.append(topic("topic-headache-localization", "Headache: pain-sensitive structures and localization", "disease-pattern",
 "The brain itself has no pain receptors; headache arises from the dura and its arteries, the venous sinuses, the large arteries at the base, the periosteum, the scalp and the cervical structures, all innervated by the trigeminal nerve above the tentorium and the upper cervical roots below it, converging on the trigeminocervical complex in the caudal medulla and upper cord. Primary headaches (migraine, tension-type, cluster and the other trigeminal autonomic cephalalgias) are disorders of this system's activation and modulation by the brainstem and hypothalamus; secondary headaches are its irritation by hemorrhage, meningitis, raised or low pressure, dissection, venous thrombosis, giant cell arteritis or mass, and recognizing the red flags that point to them is the main clinical task.",
 [("Anatomy of head pain", "The supratentorial dura, the falx and the cerebral arteries are supplied by the ophthalmic division of the trigeminal nerve (with maxillary and mandibular contributions to the middle meningeal region), so supratentorial pain is referred to the eye, forehead and temple; the posterior fossa dura and the tentorium are supplied by the upper cervical roots and the recurrent tentorial branch, so infratentorial pain is referred to the occiput and neck. Both inputs converge in the trigeminocervical complex (the spinal trigeminal nucleus and the C1-C3 dorsal horns), which explains why occipital pain accompanies migraine and why neck disease causes frontal pain. The trigeminovascular reflex releases calcitonin gene-related peptide from trigeminal terminals around the meningeal vessels, the target of modern migraine drugs, and the pain is modulated by the periaqueductal gray, the raphe and the locus coeruleus, the reason serotonergic and noradrenergic drugs prevent headache."),
  ("Primary headaches and their generators", "Migraine begins with hypothalamic and brainstem activation (premonitory yawning, hunger, mood change), sometimes an aura of cortical spreading depression that marches across the visual cortex over twenty minutes, then a throbbing unilateral headache with nausea, photophobia and phonophobia from sensitization of the trigeminovascular system and the thalamus, lasting hours to days. Cluster headache is a strictly unilateral orbital pain of 15 to 180 minutes with ipsilateral lacrimation, nasal congestion, ptosis and miosis from parasympathetic activation through the superior salivatory nucleus and sympathetic deficit, recurring at the same time of day in bouts and timed by the posterior hypothalamus; paroxysmal hemicrania and SUNCT are shorter versions with distinct treatment responses. Tension-type headache is bilateral, pressing and without autonomic features, and medication-overuse headache follows frequent analgesic use in any primary headache."),
  ("Secondary headaches and the red flags", "A thunderclap headache reaching maximum within a minute is subarachnoid hemorrhage until CT and, if negative after six hours, lumbar puncture prove otherwise, and its mimics (reversible cerebral vasoconstriction, dissection, pituitary apoplexy, venous thrombosis) need vascular imaging. A headache worse on lying, waking the patient, with vomiting, papilledema or a sixth nerve palsy means raised pressure from a mass, hydrocephalus, venous thrombosis or idiopathic intracranial hypertension; a headache that appears on standing and resolves lying down means low pressure from a CSF leak. Fever with neck stiffness means meningitis; a new headache after 50 with jaw claudication and a high sedimentation rate means giant cell arteritis and demands steroids before the biopsy; neck pain with Horner syndrome means carotid dissection; and any headache with a new focal sign, seizure or change in pattern needs imaging."),
  ("Localizing pain to a structure", "Pain around the eye with a sixth nerve palsy or ophthalmoplegia points to the cavernous sinus, the superior orbital fissure or the orbit; pain at the vertex or behind the eye with visual loss points to the pituitary region; occipital pain with neck stiffness points to the posterior fossa or the meninges; unilateral neck pain radiating to the face with a small pupil points to the carotid; trigeminal neuralgia is a brief electric pain in one division triggered by touch, usually from vascular compression at the root entry zone; occipital neuralgia is a shooting pain in the C2 distribution. Post-traumatic and cervicogenic headaches reflect the trigeminocervical convergence, and the location of a mass rarely matches the site of the pain except when it involves the dura directly.")],
 ["The brain does not hurt; the dura, the vessels and the neck do, all through the trigeminocervical complex", "Supratentorial pain is referred to the eye and forehead, infratentorial pain to the occiput and neck", "Thunderclap means subarachnoid hemorrhage until proved otherwise; the six-hour CT rule applies", "Cluster headache keeps hypothalamic time and comes with a wet, red eye and a droopy lid", "A new headache after 50 with a high sedimentation rate is giant cell arteritis; treat before the biopsy"],
 ["Ask how fast the headache reached its peak before asking where it hurts", "Positional headache means pressure: worse lying means high, worse standing means low", "Migraine with aura is a cortical event; the aura marches, the visual disturbance of a TIA does not"],
 [R("sp-acute-headache"), R("sp-migraine-headache"), R("sp-cranial-nerve-5")], meshIds=["cn-05-trigeminal-l", "cn-05-trigeminal-r", "periaqueductal-grey-l", "periaqueductal-grey-r", "hypothalamus-posterior-l", "hypothalamus-posterior-r", "sinus-cavernous-l", "sinus-cavernous-r", "falx-cerebri"],
 imaging=("Primary headaches have normal imaging; imaging is for red flags: non-contrast CT for hemorrhage, CTA or MRA for aneurysm, dissection and vasoconstriction, MRV for venous thrombosis, MRI with contrast for meningeal disease, low pressure and masses.",
  [pathol("Subarachnoid hemorrhage", "CT", "Hyperdense blood in the basal cisterns and Sylvian fissures within six hours of a thunderclap headache; CTA finds the aneurysm.", sequence="CT non-contrast", timing="CT sensitivity falls after 6 hours; lumbar puncture for xanthochromia beyond 12 hours"),
   pathol("Spontaneous intracranial hypotension", "MRI", "Diffuse smooth pachymeningeal enhancement, brain sagging with a low-lying brainstem and tonsils, subdural collections and pituitary enlargement in a patient with orthostatic headache.", sequence="T1+Gd"),
   pathol("Cerebral venous sinus thrombosis", "MRV", "Absent flow in a sinus with the empty delta sign on contrast CT or MRI and venous infarcts that do not respect arterial territories.", sequence="n/a"),
   pathol("Cervical artery dissection", "MRA", "Intramural hematoma crescent on fat-saturated T1 and tapered stenosis of the internal carotid or vertebral artery in a patient with neck pain and headache.", sequence="T1")]),
 structures=["spinal-trigeminal-nucleus-tract", "dura-mater", "tentorium-cerebelli", "sinus-cavernous", "periaqueductal-grey", "hypothalamus-posterior", "ganglion-trigeminal", "artery-internal-carotid"], syndromes=["syn-subarachnoid-hemorrhage", "syn-cerebral-venous-sinus-thrombosis", "syn-idiopathic-intracranial-hypertension", "syn-trigeminal-neuralgia", "syn-cavernous-sinus", "syn-horner"], pathways=["pathway-trigeminothalamic"], topics=["topic-csf-physiology-blood-brain-barrier", "topic-stroke-mechanisms-hemorrhage"],
 pitfalls=["A normal CT does not exclude subarachnoid hemorrhage after six hours; the lumbar puncture does", "The worst headache of a chronic migraineur can still be a hemorrhage: judge the onset, not the patient"], tags=["headache", "migraine", "trigeminovascular"]))

E.append(topic("topic-dementia-patterns", "Dementia: anatomical patterns of cognitive decline", "disease-pattern",
 "Each degenerative dementia attacks a network in a characteristic order, so the first symptoms and the pattern of atrophy on MRI localize the disease before any biomarker: Alzheimer disease begins in the entorhinal cortex and hippocampus and spreads to the posterior cingulate and parietal association cortex, producing amnesia first; frontotemporal degeneration begins in the orbitofrontal and anterior temporal cortex, producing behavioral change or semantic loss; Lewy body disease affects the brainstem, the basal forebrain and the visual association cortex, producing fluctuations, visual hallucinations and parkinsonism; and vascular cognitive impairment reflects the frontal-subcortical circuits disconnected by small vessel disease. The examination should therefore ask which cognitive domain failed first and map it onto the atlas.",
 [("Alzheimer disease: the medial temporal and parietal pattern", "Tau tangles appear first in the transentorhinal and entorhinal cortex, spread to the hippocampus and then to the temporal, parietal and frontal association cortex, while amyloid accumulates diffusely in the neocortex years before symptoms; the earliest deficit is therefore anterograde episodic memory (rapid forgetting, repeated questions, failure of delayed recall that cueing does not rescue), followed by word finding, visuospatial disorientation and executive decline. MRI shows medial temporal atrophy graded on coronal images, then parietal atrophy, and FDG-PET shows posterior cingulate and precuneus hypometabolism; CSF shows low amyloid-beta 42 and high phosphorylated tau, and amyloid and tau PET confirm the pathology. Atypical variants attack the same pathology to different networks: posterior cortical atrophy (visual), logopenic aphasia (left temporo-parietal) and the frontal variant. Cholinesterase inhibitors address the basal forebrain cholinergic loss."),
  ("Frontotemporal lobar degeneration: the anterior pattern", "The behavioral variant begins with disinhibition, apathy, loss of empathy, compulsive or ritualistic behavior, hyperorality and dietary change and executive failure with relatively preserved memory and visuospatial function, reflecting orbitofrontal, anterior cingulate, insular and anterior temporal degeneration (tau or TDP-43); MRI shows frontal and anterior temporal atrophy, often asymmetric, with knife-edge gyri. Semantic dementia is asymmetric anterior temporal atrophy with fluent speech empty of meaning, loss of word comprehension and of knowledge about objects and people; progressive non-fluent aphasia is left inferior frontal and insular atrophy with effortful, agrammatic speech and apraxia of speech. Motor neuron disease, progressive supranuclear palsy and corticobasal degeneration overlap with these syndromes, and onset before 65 with a family history suggests a genetic cause."),
  ("Lewy body disease and vascular cognitive impairment", "Dementia with Lewy bodies produces fluctuating attention and alertness, recurrent well-formed visual hallucinations, REM sleep behavior disorder and parkinsonism, with visuospatial and executive deficits more than amnesia early on; alpha-synuclein spreads from the brainstem and olfactory bulb to the limbic and neocortical regions, the basal forebrain cholinergic loss is severe, and MRI shows relative preservation of the medial temporal lobes while dopamine transporter imaging shows nigrostriatal loss. Neuroleptic sensitivity is dangerous. Vascular cognitive impairment presents with slowed processing, executive dysfunction, gait apraxia, urinary urgency and emotional lability from confluent white-matter disease, lacunes and microbleeds disconnecting the frontal-subcortical circuits, or with a stepwise course after strategic infarcts of the thalamus, caudate or angular gyrus; it coexists with Alzheimer pathology in most elderly patients."),
  ("Rapidly progressive and treatable dementias", "A dementia evolving over weeks to months is a different disease: Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease (myoclonus, cortical ribboning and basal ganglia restricted diffusion on MRI, periodic sharp waves on EEG, positive real-time quaking-induced conversion in CSF), autoimmune encephalitis (limbic seizures and psychiatric change with antibodies against neuronal surface antigens), Wernicke encephalopathy, and infections such as HIV, syphilis and Whipple disease. Treatable contributors to any dementia include normal-pressure hydrocephalus (gait first, then incontinence, then cognition), hypothyroidism, vitamin B12 deficiency, depression, sleep apnea, subdural hematoma and, above all, drugs with anticholinergic and sedative action. The workup of every dementia therefore includes imaging, thyroid function, B12, a drug review and a mood assessment, and a rapidly progressive course adds CSF, EEG and autoimmune serology.")],
 ["Ask which domain failed first: memory (Alzheimer), behavior or language (frontotemporal), attention and vision with hallucinations (Lewy), speed and gait (vascular)", "Medial temporal atrophy on coronal MRI is the structural signature of Alzheimer disease; frontal and anterior temporal atrophy of frontotemporal degeneration", "Cueing rescues retrieval in subcortical and frontal dementias but not the storage failure of Alzheimer disease", "Dementia with Lewy bodies fluctuates, hallucinates and moves badly; do not give it a neuroleptic", "Weeks-to-months dementia is prion, autoimmune or metabolic until proved otherwise"],
 ["A dementia workup that omits the drug list misses the commonest reversible cause", "Gait before memory means normal-pressure hydrocephalus; test with a large-volume tap", "Semantic dementia patients ask what a word means; Alzheimer patients ask the same question twice"],
 [R("sp-alzheimer-disease"), R("sp-frontotemporal-lobe-dementia"), R("sp-lewy-body-dementia")], meshIds=["hippocampus-l", "hippocampus-r", "gyrus-parahippocampal-anterior-l", "gyrus-parahippocampal-anterior-r", "gyrus-cingulate-posterior-l", "gyrus-cingulate-posterior-r", "precuneus-l", "precuneus-r", "cortex-orbitofrontal-l", "cortex-orbitofrontal-r", "temporal-pole-l", "temporal-pole-r", "nucleus-basalis-meynert-l", "nucleus-basalis-meynert-r"],
 imaging=("Volumetric T1 for regional atrophy (medial temporal, parietal, frontal, anterior temporal), FLAIR for vascular burden, SWI for microbleeds, DWI for prion disease, FDG-PET for the metabolic pattern and amyloid or tau PET and CSF biomarkers for Alzheimer pathology; dopamine transporter SPECT for Lewy body disease.",
  [pathol("Alzheimer disease", "MRI", "Bilateral hippocampal and entorhinal atrophy with widened temporal horns and parietal atrophy; FDG-PET hypometabolism of the posterior cingulate, precuneus and temporo-parietal cortex.", sequence="T1"),
   pathol("Behavioral variant frontotemporal dementia", "MRI", "Frontal and anterior temporal atrophy, often asymmetric, with relative sparing of the posterior cortex; FDG-PET frontal hypometabolism.", sequence="T1"),
   pathol("Dementia with Lewy bodies", "SPECT", "Reduced striatal dopamine transporter uptake with relatively preserved medial temporal volume; FDG-PET occipital hypometabolism with the cingulate island sign.", sequence="n/a"),
   pathol("Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease", "MRI", "Cortical ribbon and striatal restricted diffusion, sometimes thalamic (pulvinar sign in variant disease), with periodic sharp-wave complexes on EEG.", sequence="DWI"),
   pathol("Vascular cognitive impairment", "MRI", "Confluent periventricular and deep white-matter hyperintensities, lacunes, microbleeds and strategic infarcts of the thalamus or caudate.", sequence="FLAIR")]),
 structures=["hippocampus", "gyrus-parahippocampal-anterior", "gyrus-cingulate-posterior", "precuneus", "cortex-orbitofrontal", "temporal-pole", "nucleus-basalis-meynert", "cerebral-white-matter", "thalamus"], syndromes=["syn-normal-pressure-hydrocephalus", "syn-wernicke-korsakoff", "syn-parkinsonism", "syn-subdural-hematoma"], topics=["topic-neurotransmitter-systems", "topic-movement-disorder-circuits", "topic-sleep-eeg-basics"],
 pitfalls=["Depression in the elderly impairs attention and retrieval and mimics dementia; treat it and retest before labeling", "Anticholinergic burden from bladder, sleep and allergy drugs produces a reversible dementia that no scan will show"], tags=["dementia", "Alzheimer", "frontotemporal", "Lewy body"]))

write(E)
